A||||7||bigwave||WS6 suspension, is it worth it?||||1||
Z||000000||bigwave||12-15-2001||02:39 AM||||I'm planning on purchasing a 1988 Fiero and putting a SC 3800 in it. What would be the advantages of using a GT or Formula over the standard coupe, discounting the motor which will be replaced anyway. Is the WS6 Suspension of the V6 models vastly superior to the std coupe version? How hard would it be to swap this over to the coupe? Are all the attaching points for the rear sway bar still present on the coupe making it a simple bolt on procedure? I already plan to replace the springs and shocks, so are there any other pieces involved in this swap, other than a different front and a rear sway bar?<BR>Thanks||||reg||1
Z||000001||artherd||12-15-2001||08:17 AM||||As you will be replacing the springs, then NO, the WS6/non-gt suspension will not matter.  WS6 is just stiffer springs.<P>The rear bar attach points (holes in the cradle) are there I belive.  You will need a bar; the steel straps to hold the bar onto the cradle, and the endlinks to attach the bar onto the suspension members.<P>Consider purchasing a larger aftermarket front/rear bar combo at this time, as you need to buy a rear anyway...<P>I think that's it, otherwise just grab a cheap 88 coupe in good shape and go to town!<P>(good idea infact, with only 92bhp, the coupe will probally be in less-beat-up condition)<P>PS: The trannies in the 4-bangers are different, Isusu 5-speed in 4-cyl, Getrag 5-speed in 6-cyl.<P>The Getrag is supposedly stronger, but many V8 guys (including V8 Archie's personal cars) have 300hp+ (sometimes a BIG plus) V8s behind Isusus with no problems.<P>Best!<BR>Ben.<P><BR> <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by bigwave:<BR><B>I'm planning on purchasing a 1988 Fiero and putting a SC 3800 in it. What would be the advantages of using a GT or Formula over the standard coupe, discounting the motor which will be replaced anyway. Is the WS6 Suspension of the V6 models vastly superior to the std coupe version? How hard would it be to swap this over to the coupe? Are all the attaching points for the rear sway bar still present on the coupe making it a simple bolt on procedure? I already plan to replace the springs and shocks, so are there any other pieces involved in this swap, other than a different front and a rear sway bar?<BR>Thanks</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P><P>------------------<BR>      <IMG SRC="http://www.cartsys.com/fiero/sig1.jpeg">    <BR>Ben Cannon<BR>88 Formula, T-top, Metalic Red           &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;"Every Man Dies, not every man really Lives"<BR>88 Formula, Northstar, Silver                    &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;-Mel Gibson, "Braveheart"||||reg||3
Z||000002||theogre||12-15-2001||01:57 PM||||WS6 didn't mean much back then. It's even more meaningless now. If someone is using WS6 as a selling point... save your money.<P>Stiffer springs... maybe better, maybe not. You're adding some weight with a V6 upgrade so they should help. Depends partly what springs are in there now. Somewhere here there was a post that listed the spring codes so you could tell which set you had.<P>Good shocks and struts are a must. These can make more differance than springs often do. You want gas units with velocity sensitive valves. Nearly all of the better products have this now. These will stiffen considerably on a big bump w/o sacrificing a good ride. Remember, reducing the wheel travel passing over a bump also reduces bump steer that some many people claim plagues Fiero.<P>One of the things I noticed when I removed the OE struts from mine... they had no bump (compression) damping, only rebound (expansion). I'm reasonably sure GM bought them that way. (Cheep Cheep Cheep, I bet even WS6 got them.)<P>The new Gabriels I put on have damping in both directions. Made a huge differance in handling. Gabriel's VST valve lets the car ride decent but hold the wheels under control even when crossing rail road tracks that are angled. ||||reg||1
Z||000003||speed racer||12-15-2001||04:46 PM||||I have an 87 GT and an 86 SE how would I know if they had the WS6 sustention or not.<BR>Kyle||||reg||1
Z||000004||theogre||12-15-2001||05:19 PM||||RPO code WS6 will be on the lable by the brake master cylinder up front.||||reg||1
Z||000005||ray b||12-15-2001||05:27 PM||||were any GT's made without WS6????<BR>all forumlas have it tooo<BR>are there codes that hide WS6 in them???/<P>------------------<BR>Question wonder and be wierd||||reg||1
Z||000006||theogre||12-15-2001||11:39 PM||||WS6 is WS6.... There are other handling packages besides WS6. It was an option on a limited number of cars. <P>The indivirual spring codes are also in the RPO label on most modles. You can look all that up in the RPO lists avaiable on some of the club sites and other places. I don't have any links to them handy.... there is also a list somewhere around here of just the spring codes so you know what set you have relative to the other sets available..... No, I don't have the link for that either. <p>[This message has been edited by theogre (edited 12-15-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000007||cadero2dmax||12-17-2001||08:38 PM||||Nobody has mentioned that the standard suspension did not include a rear swaybar - a very important improvement for handling.<P>------------------<BR>George Ryan<BR>A pair of 4.9 Cadero's<P>For Racing Photos of my former Q4 Fiero racecar, go to: <BR>http://albums.photopoint.com/j/AlbumIndex?u=142520&a=1342821 <P>(until I can figure out how to post photos)||||reg||1
